{
  "sim_type": "excel",
  "title": "Reliance Industries Balance Sheet and Cash Flow Statement Analysis",
  "scenario": "You're a junior analyst at a Mumbai-based equity desk reviewing Reliance Industries' latest balance sheet and cash flow statement (FY2024). Your senior asks you to verify the balance sheet equation, trace key working capital movements, and reconcile closing cash from the cash flow statement to the balance sheet. This is your first test of understanding how the three statements lock together.",
  "instructions": "Complete the balance sheet verification, calculate working capital changes, and build a simple cash flow reconciliation to prove that closing cash from operations plus capex and financing equals the balance sheet's cash balance.",
  "learning_objective": "Verify balance sheet equilibrium, calculate working capital movements (inventory, receivables, payables), and reconcile closing cash from the cash flow statement to the balance sheet cash line to confirm the three statements lock together.",
  "hints": [
    "Start by checking Assets = Liabilities + Equity using simple addition. Then identify which rows are current assets and current liabilities — these feed the working capital section.",
    "Working capital changes reverse the balance sheet sign: if receivables went up from ₹1000 cr to ₹1100 cr, that's a +₹100 cr outflow (asset up eats cash). Payables up from ₹800 cr to ₹850 cr is a +₹50 cr inflow (liability up saves cash).",
    "The cash reconciliation formula is: Opening Cash + CFO + CFI + CFF = Closing Cash. Your Closing Cash number from this formula must match exactly the 'Cash and bank balances' line on the balance sheet — this is the lock."
  ],
